## Appendix B Magnetic Flux and Flux Ratio Distributions

To determine the distribution of magnetic flux \\Phi_{\\rm BH,eq} and flux ratio \\Delta_{\\rm eq} at spin equilibrium for a given M_{\\rm acc}, we must assume a range for \\Delta_{\\rm eq}, as the curve in Figure 5 is not monotonic. For \\Delta_{\\rm eq}\\gtrsim 0.04, two values of \\Delta_{\\rm eq} correspond to the same normalized spin equilibrium jet power, complicating inversion. Assuming that all IGRBs originate from systems where \\Delta_{\\rm eq}<\\Delta_{\\rm eq,max} allows a one-to-one mapping between observed jet energy, E_{\\rm jet}, and flux ratio \\Delta_{\\rm eq}. Figure 6 (left) shows \\Delta_{\\rm eq} distributions for various M_{\\rm acc} values relative to the observed jet energy distribution. As M_{\\rm acc} increases, the system deviates from the MAD regime, but even for M_{\\rm acc}=0.2M_{\\odot}, the peak of \\Delta_{\\rm eq} remains below its maximum, aligning with our assumption that \\Delta_{\\rm eq}<\\Delta_{\\rm eq,max} across all systems.

The MAD flux limit \\Phi_{\\rm MAD,eq}(a_{\\rm eq},\\dot{m},M_{\\rm BH}) is primarily influenced by the mass accretion rate at a given \\Delta_{\\rm eq}, as M_{\\rm BH} remains almost constant during the burst, and a_{\\rm eq} depends solely on \\Delta_{\\rm eq}. We approximate \\dot{m}\\approx M_{\\rm acc}/T_{90}, where T_{90} is distributed as \\log_{10}T_{90}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0.94,0.464)(Zitouni et al., 2018), based on _Fermi_ GRBs. Assuming this distribution is independent of jet luminosity, we derive the distribution of \\Phi_{\\rm BH,eq}, shown in Figure 6 (right) shows the distributions of \\Phi_{\\rm BH,eq} for M_{\\rm acc}=0.2,1,5M_{\\odot}. While the \\Delta_{\\rm eq} distributions do not overlap, as \\dot{m} increases with M_{\\rm acc}, the \\Phi_{\\rm BH,eq} distributions overlap. This is because most systems are far from MAD, with a_{\\rm eq}\\sim 1, so the magnetic flux distributions closely follow the luminosity distribution. For M_{\\rm acc}=0.2M_{\\odot}, however, the high-flux tail should have a_{\\rm eq}<1 due to \\Delta_{\\rm eq}>0.1, leading to a divergence from the other distributions. Our results remain largely unaffected when using alternative T_{90} distributions, such as those derived from _Swift_.

For sGRBs, assuming E_{\\rm jet}=L_{\\rm jet}T_{90}, we find from Equation 1 that

E_{\\rm jet}=\\frac{\\kappa f}{4\\pi c}\\Phi_{\\rm BH}^{2}\\Omega_{\\rm H}^{2}(a=0.7, M_{\\rm BH,0})\\,T_{90} (B4)

Solving for \\Phi_{\\rm BH} gives

\\begin{split}\\Phi_{\\rm BH}&=2.77\\times 10^{27} \\left(\\frac{M_{\\rm BH,0}}{2.5M_{\\odot}}\\right)\\times\\\\ &\\qquad\\left(\\frac{E_{\\rm jet}}{10^{50}{\\rm erg}}\\right)^{1/2} \\left(\\frac{T_{90}}{0.2{\\rm s}}\\right)^{-1/2}[{\\rm Gauss\\ cm}^{2}].\\end{split} (B5)

From Equation 10, the flux ratio \\Delta=\\Phi_{\\rm BH}/\\Phi_{\\rm MAD} becomes

\\begin{split}\\Delta&=\\frac{\\Phi_{\\rm BH}}{\\Phi_{ \\rm MAD}}\\\\ &=0.028\\left(\\frac{E_{\\rm jet}}{10^{50}{\\rm erg}}\\right)^{1/2} \\left(\\frac{T_{90}}{0.2{\\rm s}}\\right)^{-1/2}\\left(\\frac{\\dot{m}}{0.5M_{\\odot }/{\\rm s}}\\right)^{-1/2}.\\end{split} (B6)

For typical sGRB energies and durations, \\Phi_{\\rm BH} is far from the MAD limit. Figure 7 shows the distributions of \\Phi_{\\rm BH} for sGRBs, assuming \\log_{10}T_{90}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(-0.62,0.442)(Zitouni et al., 2018) and various distributions of \\log_{10}(E_{\\rm jet}). The \\Phi_{\\rm BH} distributions for sGRBs closely resemble those of IGRBs due to similar luminosity distributions and high BH spin values during the bursts, and this similarity is largely insensitive to the specific choice of E_{\\rm jet} distribution, as long as it is reasonable. Again, we use the _Fermi_ GRB distribution for \\log_{10}T_{90}, though the results are not sensitive to this specific choice. The magnetic flux distributions for both long and short GRBs peak around 10^{27} G cm{}^{2}, indicating a potential common origin of magnetic flux in both central engine systems.